<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!-- 
      ===================================================================================================
      INTERVIEW PREPARATION: POM.XML EXPLANATION
      ===================================================================================================
      What is this file? 
      This is the Project Object Model (POM), the core configuration file for Maven.
      It tells Maven how to build the project, what dependencies it needs, and what plugins to run.

      Key sections for this microservice:
      1. <parent>: Inherits versions/configs from `ecommerce-system-design`. This means we don't 
         need to specify versions for Spring Cloud/Boot dependencies here (dependency management).
      2. <dependencies>: The required libraries. They follow a specific layered architecture:
         - Web Layer: Spring Web (Tomcat, MVC)
         - Data Layer: Spring Data JPA, MySQL Driver
         - Service Discovery & Routing: Eureka Client, Feign, LoadBalancer
         - Resilience: Resilience4j (Circuit Breakers)
         - Observability: Actuator, Micrometer (Prometheus), OpenTelemetry (Tracing)
         - Tooling: Lombok (Boilerplate), SpringDoc (Swagger UI)
      3. <build>: Specifies the `spring-boot-maven-plugin` which creates an executable "fat JAR" 
         containing all dependencies and an embedded Tomcat server.
      ===================================================================================================
    -->

    <parent>
        <groupId>com.ecommerce</groupId>
        <artifactId>ecommerce-system-design</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>user-service</artifactId>
    <name>user-service</name>

    <!--
    ==========================================
    INTERVIEW NOTES - USER SERVICE (pom.xml)
    ==========================================

    Q: What is the role of the User Service in a microservices architecture?
    A: The User Service is a domain-specific microservice responsible for user
       registration, login, profile management, and fetching user-linked orders
       by calling the Order Service via OpenFeign. It owns its own MySQL database
       (Database-per-Service pattern) and registers with Eureka for discovery.

    Q: How does User Service differ from Order Service in terms of dependencies?
    A: Key differences:
       - NO Kafka: User Service does not publish or consume async events.
         User operations are synchronous (register, login, get profile).
       - NO AOP starter: No cross-cutting concerns like method-level metrics or
         custom audit aspects are implemented here.
       - HAS OpenFeign: User Service calls Order Service synchronously to fetch
         orders belonging to a user (cross-service query).

    Q: Why inherit from a parent POM?
    A: The parent POM (ecommerce-system-design) centralizes:
       - Dependency version management (no version duplication in child modules)
       - Common plugin configuration (spring-boot-maven-plugin)
       - Shared properties (Java version, Spring Boot/Cloud BOM versions)
       This follows the DRY principle and prevents version conflicts across services.
    -->

    <dependencies>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        1. CORE WEB - spring-boot-starter-web
        ==========================================
        Q: What does spring-boot-starter-web provide?
        A: Bundles Spring MVC, an embedded Tomcat server, Jackson (JSON serialization),
           and auto-configuration. Enables building RESTful APIs with @RestController.

        Q: Why embedded Tomcat instead of a standalone server?
        A: Simplifies deployment (fat JAR), aligns with 12-Factor App principles,
           and is ideal for containerized microservices (Docker/K8s).

        Q: What is the difference between @Controller and @RestController?
        A: @RestController = @Controller + @ResponseBody. It serializes return values
           directly to JSON/XML in the HTTP response body. @Controller is used for
           view-based (Thymeleaf/JSP) responses.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        2. ORM / DATABASE - spring-boot-starter-data-jpa
        ==========================================
        Q: What is Spring Data JPA and why use it?
        A: Provides Hibernate ORM on top of JPA. Enables @Entity mapping and
           repository pattern (JpaRepository) - eliminates boilerplate CRUD code.
           Supports JPQL and native queries.

        Q: Database-per-service pattern?
        A: Each microservice (order, user, payment) has its OWN dedicated MySQL
           schema. Ensures loose coupling - no shared DB, no cross-service JOINs.
           Services query each other only via APIs or events.

        Q: What is the N+1 query problem in JPA?
        A: When fetching a list of entities (1 query), JPA lazily fetches each
           related entity separately (N queries). Fix: use JOIN FETCH in JPQL,
           @EntityGraph, or @BatchSize to load associations eagerly in one query.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        3. INPUT VALIDATION - spring-boot-starter-validation
        ==========================================
        Q: What does this provide?
        A: Integrates Hibernate Validator (Bean Validation 3.0 / JSR-380).
           Enables annotations like @NotNull, @NotBlank, @Min, @Max, @Email
           on DTOs and method parameters with @Valid / @Validated.

        Q: Why validate at the service boundary (controller layer)?
        A: Prevents invalid data from reaching the business layer or DB.
           Returns structured 400 Bad Request responses early, reducing load.

        Q: Difference between @Valid and @Validated?
        A: @Valid: standard JSR-380, used on method parameters for basic validation.
           @Validated: Spring-specific, supports validation groups and can be used
           on class level to enable method-level validation via AOP proxy.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        4. SERVICE DISCOVERY - spring-cloud-starter-netflix-eureka-client
        ==========================================
        Q: What is Eureka and why is it needed?
        A: Eureka is a Service Registry (Netflix OSS). Each microservice registers
           itself on startup with its host/port. Other services discover it by name
           (e.g., "user-service") instead of hardcoded IPs - enabling dynamic scaling.

        Q: What happens if Eureka goes down?
        A: Eureka clients cache the registry locally. Services continue to communicate
           using the cached list (self-preservation mode). For production, Eureka is
           deployed in a cluster (multiple instances).

        Q: How does the User Service use Eureka specifically?
        A: The User Service registers as "user-service". The API Gateway discovers
           it via Eureka to route incoming requests. The User Service also uses Eureka
           to resolve "order-service" when making Feign calls to fetch user orders.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-netflix-eureka-client</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        5. HTTP CLIENT - spring-cloud-starter-openfeign
        ==========================================
        Q: What is OpenFeign?
        A: A declarative HTTP client. You define an interface annotated with
           @FeignClient("service-name") and Spring generates the implementation.
           Eliminates manual RestTemplate/WebClient boilerplate.

        Q: Why does User Service need Feign?
        A: User Service calls Order Service to fetch orders for a given user.
           e.g., GET /orders?userId=123 on the Order Service is called from
           UserService via @FeignClient("order-service").

        Q: What is the difference between Feign and RestTemplate?
        A: Feign is declarative (interface-based, less code), supports Ribbon/LoadBalancer
           natively, and integrates with Resilience4j for circuit breaking.
           RestTemplate is imperative and requires manual URL construction.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-openfeign</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        6. CLIENT-SIDE LOAD BALANCING - spring-cloud-starter-loadbalancer
        ==========================================
        Q: What does Spring Cloud LoadBalancer do?
        A: Replaces Netflix Ribbon (deprecated). Performs client-side load balancing -
           when multiple instances of a service are registered in Eureka, it picks one
           using Round Robin (default) or custom strategy.

        Q: Client-side vs Server-side Load Balancing?
        A: Client-side: the caller (User Service) picks the target instance.
           Server-side: a separate LB (e.g., AWS ALB, Nginx) routes traffic.
           Client-side reduces infrastructure complexity in microservices.

        Q: Is LoadBalancer required with Feign?
        A: Yes. When Feign resolves a service name (e.g., "order-service"), it delegates
           to Spring Cloud LoadBalancer to pick the actual instance from the Eureka registry.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-loadbalancer</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        7. FAULT TOLERANCE - resilience4j-spring-boot3
        ==========================================
        Q: What is Resilience4j and what patterns does it support?
        A: A lightweight fault tolerance library. Key patterns:
           - Circuit Breaker: Stops calls to a failing service (OPEN/CLOSED/HALF-OPEN states)
           - Retry: Automatically retries failed calls N times
           - Rate Limiter: Limits calls per time window
           - Bulkhead: Limits concurrent calls to prevent thread exhaustion
           - Time Limiter: Sets a max duration for a call

        Q: Where is Resilience4j used in the User Service?
        A: On the Feign client that calls the Order Service. If Order Service is slow or
           down, the Circuit Breaker opens and returns a fallback (e.g., empty list)
           instead of propagating failures to the user-facing API.

        Q: What are the Circuit Breaker states?
        A: CLOSED (normal) -> OPEN (after failure threshold, fast-fail) ->
           HALF-OPEN (test calls) -> back to CLOSED or OPEN based on results.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.github.resilience4j</groupId>
            <artifactId>resilience4j-spring-boot3</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        8. OBSERVABILITY - spring-boot-starter-actuator
        ==========================================
        Q: What does Actuator provide?
        A: Production-ready endpoints for monitoring:
           /actuator/health     -> liveness and readiness probes (K8s)
           /actuator/metrics    -> JVM, HTTP, custom metrics
           /actuator/info       -> build/git metadata
           /actuator/env        -> environment properties

        Q: Why is /actuator/health critical in microservices?
        A: Kubernetes uses it for liveness/readiness probes. If health check fails,
           K8s restarts the pod or removes it from the load balancer.

        Q: What is the difference between liveness and readiness probes?
        A: Liveness: Is the app alive? If no, restart the container.
           Readiness: Is the app ready to serve traffic? If no, remove from LB pool.
           Spring Actuator exposes both under /actuator/health/liveness and /readiness.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        9. METRICS - micrometer-registry-prometheus
        ==========================================
        Q: What is Micrometer?
        A: A vendor-neutral metrics facade (like SLF4J for logging). Collects JVM,
           HTTP, and custom metrics and exports them in Prometheus format.

        Q: How does the Prometheus + Grafana stack work?
        A: Prometheus scrapes /actuator/prometheus every N seconds -> stores time-series
           data -> Grafana queries Prometheus and renders dashboards (latency, error rate,
           throughput - RED metrics).

        Q: What are RED metrics?
        A: Rate (requests/sec), Errors (failed requests/sec), Duration (latency).
           Core SLIs for microservice health monitoring.

        Q: What are the default metrics exposed by Micrometer?
        A: http.server.requests (latency, status codes), jvm.memory.used,
           jvm.gc.pause, system.cpu.usage, hikaricp.connections (DB pool).
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
            <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        10. DISTRIBUTED TRACING - micrometer-tracing-bridge-otel
        ==========================================
        Q: What is distributed tracing and why is it needed?
        A: In microservices, a single user request spans multiple services. Distributed
           tracing assigns a unique Trace ID and Span IDs to correlate logs across services.
           Enables end-to-end latency analysis and root cause identification.

        Q: How does tracing work in the User Service?
        A: When a request enters User Service, a Trace ID is generated (or propagated
           from the API Gateway via W3C TraceContext headers). Each outgoing Feign call
           to Order Service carries this Trace ID, linking both spans in Jaeger/Tempo.

        Q: What is the OpenTelemetry bridge here?
        A: micrometer-tracing-bridge-otel bridges Micrometer's tracing API to the
           OpenTelemetry SDK - making it vendor-neutral (can export to Jaeger, Zipkin,
           Tempo, etc.).
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
            <artifactId>micrometer-tracing-bridge-otel</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        11. TRACE EXPORTER - opentelemetry-exporter-otlp
        ==========================================
        Q: What does the OTLP exporter do?
        A: Exports trace spans using the OpenTelemetry Protocol (OTLP) to a collector
           (e.g., Grafana Tempo, Jaeger, OpenTelemetry Collector). OTLP is the modern
           standard, replacing Zipkin's B3 format.

        Q: Trace vs Span relationship?
        A: A Trace = one end-to-end request (unique Trace ID).
           A Span = one unit of work within a trace (e.g., DB call, HTTP call).
           Spans are nested (parent-child) forming a trace tree.

        Q: What is sampling in distributed tracing?
        A: Not every request needs to be traced (overhead). Sampling controls what
           fraction of requests are traced. Common strategies:
           - Head-based: decision at trace start (e.g., 10% of requests)
           - Tail-based: decision after span completes (e.g., always trace errors)
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.opentelemetry</groupId>
            <artifactId>opentelemetry-exporter-otlp</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        12. API DOCUMENTATION - springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui
        ==========================================
        Q: What is SpringDoc OpenAPI?
        A: Auto-generates OpenAPI 3.0 (Swagger) documentation by scanning
           @RestController, @RequestMapping, @Schema annotations.
           Provides Swagger UI at /swagger-ui.html for interactive API testing.

        Q: Why is explicit version defined here?
        A: springdoc-openapi is a third-party library - not managed by Spring Boot's
           BOM. So the version must be explicitly declared (2.5.0 supports Boot 3.x).

        Q: OpenAPI 3.0 vs Swagger 2.0?
        A: OpenAPI 3.0 (successor to Swagger 2.0) supports oneOf/anyOf schemas,
           multiple servers, improved security schemes, and request/response examples.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
            <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
            <version>2.5.0</version>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        13. TESTING - spring-boot-starter-test (scope: test)
        ==========================================
        Q: What does spring-boot-starter-test include?
        A: JUnit 5, Mockito, AssertJ, MockMvc (for controller layer testing),
           @SpringBootTest (integration tests), @DataJpaTest (JPA slice tests).

        Q: What is scope="test"?
        A: Dependency is only on the test classpath - not included in the production
           fat JAR. Reduces artifact size and prevents accidental production usage.

        Q: How do you test a Feign client without a real server?
        A: Use @SpringBootTest with WireMock (stub HTTP server) or MockBean the Feign
           interface with Mockito to unit test the service layer in isolation.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        14. DATABASE DRIVER - mysql-connector-j (scope: runtime)
        ==========================================
        Q: Why scope="runtime" for the MySQL driver?
        A: The code only uses JDBC/JPA interfaces (compile-time). The actual MySQL
           driver implementation is only needed at runtime. This is a best practice
           for JDBC drivers - keeps compile classpath clean.

        Q: Why MySQL for User Service?
        A: User data is relational (User -> Address -> Roles). ACID transactions
           ensure data integrity (e.g., user registration is atomic).
           MySQL provides strong consistency, JOINs, and foreign key constraints.

        Q: What is HikariCP and why does Spring Boot use it?
        A: HikariCP is the default connection pool in Spring Boot. It maintains a
           pool of reusable DB connections, avoiding the cost of creating a new
           connection per request. Fastest JDBC connection pool available.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.mysql</groupId>
            <artifactId>mysql-connector-j</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        ==========================================
        15. BOILERPLATE REDUCTION - lombok (optional: true)
        ==========================================
        Q: What does Lombok provide?
        A: Annotation-based code generation at compile time:
           @Getter/@Setter, @Builder, @NoArgsConstructor, @AllArgsConstructor,
           @Data (all of the above), @Slf4j (injects a logger field).

        Q: Why optional="true"?
        A: Means Lombok is NOT transitively inherited by modules depending on this one.
           It's a compile-time tool - not needed at runtime or by consumers.

        Q: How does Lombok work under the hood?
        A: Uses Java Annotation Processing (APT) - plugs into the compiler and
           generates bytecode before the main compilation step. Nothing is added
           to the runtime classpath; Lombok's output is plain Java code.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId>
            <artifactId>lombok</artifactId>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>repackage</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
